---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `local ship

function love.load()
  ship = love.graphics.newImage("ship.png")
end

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.draw(ship, 400, 300)
end`;

const Figure2 = `love.graphics.draw(image, x, y, rotation, scaleX, scaleY, originX, originY)`;

const Figure3 = `-- top-left of the image lands at 400, 300
love.graphics.draw(ship, 400, 300)

-- center of the image lands at 400, 300
love.graphics.draw(ship, 400, 300, 0, 1, 1, ship:getWidth() / 2, ship:getHeight() / 2)`;

const Figure4 = `love.graphics.setColor(1, 0.3, 0.3)
love.graphics.draw(ship, 400, 300)`;

const Task1 = `-- main.lua

local ship

function love.load()
  love.graphics.setBackgroundColor(0.05, 0.05, 0.1)
  ship = love.graphics.newImage("ship.png")
end

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.setColor(1, 1, 1)
  love.graphics.draw(ship, 400, 300)
end`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua

local ship

function love.load()
  love.graphics.setBackgroundColor(0.05, 0.05, 0.1)
  ship = love.graphics.newImage("ship.png")
end

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.setColor(1, 1, 1)
  love.graphics.draw(ship, 400, 300, 0, 2, 2, ship:getWidth() / 2, ship:getHeight() / 2)
end`;

const Task3 = `-- main.lua

local ship
local angle = 0

function love.load()
  love.graphics.setBackgroundColor(0.05, 0.05, 0.1)
  ship = love.graphics.newImage("ship.png")
end

function love.update(dt)
  angle = angle + dt
end

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.setColor(1, 1, 1)
  love.graphics.draw(ship, 400, 300, angle, 2, 2, ship:getWidth() / 2, ship:getHeight() / 2)
end`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.4: Sprites and Images</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            A square is a fine ship for testing and a poor ship for playing.
            Loading a picture is two lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Download the sprite and put it in your
            skynest folder</b>, next to main.lua.
          </p>

          <p class="my-4">
            <a
              class="btn btn-outline-primary"
              href="/courses/lua-programming/ship.png"
              download="ship.png"
            >
              <i class="si-download"></i> ship.png &mdash; 32 × 32
            </a>
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">
              <CodeSnippet
                code="newImage"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              goes in{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.load"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />, never in{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.draw"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </b>{" "}
            It reads the file off disk and sends it to the graphics card. Do that
            sixty times a second and the game will crawl, then run out of memory.
            <b> Load once, draw many times</b> &mdash; the variable holds the
            loaded image, and drawing it is cheap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note that{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="ship"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is declared at the top of the file but assigned inside{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.load"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />. That is deliberate:{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="local"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            inside the function would make a variable that vanishes when it
            returns, and your draw would find{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="nil"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />. Activity 1.2 and Activity 1.8, still applying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">The full draw call</h3>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="none"
            lang="lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            Everything after{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="y"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is optional, and they must be supplied <b>in order</b> &mdash; to set
            a scale you have to pass a rotation first, even if it is 0. That is
            Lua's ordinary positional arguments, not a LÖVE rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Rotation is in radians</b>, not degrees. A full turn is{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="2 * math.pi"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />; a quarter turn is{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="math.pi / 2"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />. If you prefer degrees,{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="math.rad(90)"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            converts for you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Origin is the important one</h3>

          <p>
            By default LÖVE puts the image's <b>top-left corner</b> at the x and
            y you give it. The origin arguments move that anchor point.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            The origin does two jobs at once, which is why it matters more than
            it looks. It is <b>the point that lands on x, y</b>, and it is also{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">the point the image rotates around</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave it at the default and a rotating sprite swings around its own
            top-left corner, orbiting rather than spinning. Set it to the middle
            of the image and it turns on the spot. <b>Almost always you want the
            middle.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            Use{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="ship:getWidth() / 2"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            rather than typing 16. Swap in a bigger sprite later and the code
            still centers it. That colon is Activity 1.11's method call &mdash;
            the loaded image is an object with methods on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>One catch worth knowing now:</b> the origin is measured in the
            image's own unscaled pixels, before{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="sx"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="sy"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            are applied. So half the width is correct whatever scale you draw at,
            and you never multiply the origin by the scale yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Color tints images</h3>

          <Code
            code={Figure4}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            That draws the ship <b>red</b>. The current color is multiplied
            through the image rather than ignored, which is genuinely useful
            &mdash; it is how you flash an enemy white when it is hit, without a
            second sprite.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">It is also a reliable source of confusion.</b>{" "}
            Draw a colored shape, then draw a sprite, and the sprite comes out
            wearing the shape's color. The state machine from Activity 2.2 does
            not stop at shapes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>So set{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.graphics.setColor(1, 1, 1)"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            before drawing any image you want to look normal.</b> White is "no
            tint." Get into the habit now, because the day it bites you the
            sprite will simply be the wrong color and nothing will be an error.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Put <b class="text-sn-theme">ship.png</b> in your skynest folder
            first. If LÖVE cannot find it you will get an error naming the file,
            which is one of the few loud failures you will meet.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Load it and draw it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Declare the image variable at the top of the file, and assign it
              inside{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.load"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
            <li>Draw it at 400, 300 with no extra arguments.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Look at where it actually landed.</b> Its top-left corner is at
              the center of the window, so the sprite sits down and to the right
              of where you probably expected.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Center it and scale it up</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a rotation of <b>0</b>, a scale of <b>2</b> on both axes, and
              an origin of half the image's width and height.
            </li>
            <li>Compute the origin from the image, not from the number 16.</li>
            <li>
              <b>It should now be centered</b>, and twice the size. A 32-pixel
              sprite drawn at 2 is a comfortable 64 pixels.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "12" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Spin it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add an{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="angle"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              variable and grow it by{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="dt"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              each frame &mdash; one radian per second, so a full turn takes a
              little over six seconds.
            </li>
            <li>Pass it as the rotation.</li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Then set the two origin arguments back to
              0 and run it again.</b> The ship swings in a wide circle instead of
              turning on the spot. That is the clearest demonstration of what
              origin does, and it is worth seeing rather than being told.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "4, 11-14, 17" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Bring back Activity 2.3's movement and draw the sprite at the
              ship's position instead of a square. <b>This is the version you
              keep</b> &mdash; the rest of the unit builds on it.
            </li>
            <li>
              Make the ship <b>tilt</b> as it moves: set the angle to a small
              positive number while moving right and a small negative one while
              moving left, and back to 0 otherwise. It costs four lines and makes
              the ship feel alive.
            </li>
            <li>
              Tint the ship red while any key is held, using{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="setColor"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              before the draw. Then remove the{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="setColor(1, 1, 1)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              afterwards and watch the tint leak into everything else you draw.
            </li>
            <li>
              Flip the sprite by passing <b>-2</b> as the horizontal scale. Work
              out why the origin makes that flip in place rather than jumping
              sideways.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              A blue arrowhead ship, <b>64 pixels</b> across, exactly in the
              middle of the window.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Turning steadily on the spot</b>, one
              rotation every six or so seconds.
            </li>
            <li>Its colors unchanged &mdash; blue hull, pale cockpit.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            If it orbits rather than spins, the origin arguments are missing or
            zero. If it is tiny, the scale is missing. If it is a strange color,
            something set a color before the draw and never set it back. And if
            LÖVE refused to start, the file is not in the folder or the name does
            not match &mdash; that one is case-sensitive on most machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>That is the first block.</b> You have a window, a loop, shapes,
            input, frame-rate-independent movement, and a sprite. Activity 2.5
            starts the game proper, with a table of bullets &mdash; and it opens
            by walking a list backwards, exactly as Activity 1.9 said it would.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-light"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-2/2-3"
          >
            <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Previous
          </a>
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-primary"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-2/2-5"
          >
            Next: Many Things at Once <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
